{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated source-reviewer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Review only scientific validity and inference scope. Return {\"findings\":[{\"check_id\":\"CITE-CONTEXT|SCOPE-INFLATE|ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK|RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\"severity\":\"info|minor|major|critical\",\"evidence\":[{\"id\":str,\"locator\":str,\"sha256\":str}],\"recommendation\":str}],\"summary\":str}. Use major only when the direction result cannot be admitted without repair.\nINPUT:\n{\"direction\": {\"title\": \"Regolith Construction Trade Study\", \"rationale\": \"Compare processing routes under energy, mass, strength, and maintenance constraints to identify viable pathways for in-situ resource utilization.\", \"workflow\": \"engineering_design\", \"completion_test\": \"A requirements matrix and bounded candidate comparison that authorizes selection of a baseline architecture for prototyping.\", \"remaining\": \"Mars material tests and prototypes to validate structural performance and scalability.\", \"position\": 0}, \"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427\", \"sha256\": \"1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427\", \"title\": \"Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction - ScienceDirect\", \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352710224018138\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 26}, \"content\": \"Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction - ScienceDirect Skip to main contentSkip to article\\n## Journal of Building Engineering Date: 15 October 2024 Article: 110245 ## Published by: Elsevier ### Published by Show more Research article Get rights and\\ncontent # Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction Charun Bao, Peng Feng, Daobo Zhang, Qinyu\\nWang, Shaobo Yang Show more Add to Mendeley More actions ## Highlights ## Keywords Lunar habitat ; In situ construction ; Regolith bag ; Structural design ;\\nMultiscale experimental testing - Previous article in this issue - Next article in this issue ## Introduction The aspiration to construct lunar habitats and\\nachieve long-term residency has always fueled humanity's drive. As the closest celestial body to Earth and the gateway to deep space exploration for humans, the\\nmoon holds significant material, environmental, and positional resources. Establishing permanent lunar habitats is an inevitable choice for lunar scientific\\nresearch, resource development, and maintaining space peace and stability. In 2019, the United States introduced the Artemis Program, aiming to establish a\\nsustainable presence on the lunar surface within the next decade [1]. In 2021, China and Russia unveiled their plan to construct the International Lunar\\nResearch Station (ILRS), focusing on creating scientific research facilities in the lunar polar regions [2,3]. Additionally, Europe, Japan, South Korea, and\\nIndia have also announced their intentions for lunar exploration [[4], [5], [6], [7]]. Consequently, the construction of lunar habitats has emerged as a crucial\\nengineering requirement and technical challenge in lunar exploration missions. Lunar construction poses formidable challenges, primarily in transporting\\nmaterials from the Earth to the moon. This endeavor demands substantial resources, including finances, energy, time, and effort. Other harsh conditions,\\ncharacterized by limited energy supplies, the lack of mechanical equipment, and the absence of human assistance, compound the complexity of lunar construction.\\nMoreover, the extreme lunar environments introduce unique structural loading considerations. The vacuum environment induces substantial pressure differences in\\ninternal and external environments, imposing significant tensile loads on structures. This is a common issue in space structures. Furthermore, lunar temperature\\nvariation far exceeds that experienced on the Earth, exposing structures to considerable thermal stresses. In addition, lunar structures must cope with\\nheightened radiation levels compared to Earth. Another unique environmental problem is the abrasive and charged characteristics of lunar dust, driven by space\\nweathering and solar wind. Furthermore, although rare, micrometeorites and moonquakes introduce potential threats to structures. Micrometeorite impact may cause\\nstructural air leaks in a vacuum environment, resulting in catastrophic consequences. In summary, the construction conditions and service environment present\\ndistinctive and formidable challenges. Extensive research has been devoted to the construction of inhabited buildings in lunar habitats, as they are regarded as\\nthe most crucial and complex construction projects. Over the past six decades, the conceptual scheme for lunar inhabited buildings undergone significant\\ndevelopment, primarily shifting from Earth-based prefabrication to in situ construction on the moon. Early construction schemes are generally centered around\\nEarth prefabricated structures, such as NASA's LESA integrated module proposed in the 1960s. This module, weighing over 11 tons, was designed to be launched to\\nthe moon using the Saturn V Rocket [8]. Similarly, the Soviet Union also proposed a module construction scheme in the Soviet Union Space Projects, planning to\\nlaunch nine prefabricated m\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"title\": \"On the Structural Design and Additive Construction Process of Martian Habitat Units Using In-Situ Resources on Mars\", \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2226-4310/12/9/761\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_upstream_error\\nTarget returned an unsupported response.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"title\": \"Autonomous Robot Swarms for Off-World Construction and Resource Mining\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2514/6.2020-0795\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_upstream_error\\nTarget returned an unsupported response.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791\", \"sha256\": \"9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791\", \"title\": \"Sustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete | Scientific Reports\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 540}, \"content\": \"## Sustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete | Scientific Reports\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#content)\\n\\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\\nand JavaScript.\\n\\nSustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete\\n\\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9.pdf)\\n\\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9.pdf)\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThe major obstacle to Martian colonization is the mission cost which requires significant reduction. From the structural engineering point of view, importing\\nmaterials and structural elements from Earth or massive excavations on the surface of Mars require an enormous amount of energy; thus, inflatable and under-\\nsurface structures as the main options for Martian colonization seem unrealistically expensive. Construction of affordable buildings onsite using only in situ\\nsources may represent an ideal solution for Martian colonization. On the other hand, solar energy, at the early stage of colonization, would be the only\\navailable, practical, and low-cost energy source on Mars. Though, for sustainable and broad colonization, the energy required for construction and the\\nconstruction cost should be minimized. Here, we propose three types of simple (relatively optimized), perforated, and algorithmic shape-optimized Martian\\nstructures to minimize the material and energy required for construction as well as the construction cost using only in situ resources. These structural forms\\ncan be considered remarkable steps towards sustainable structural construction and colonization on Mars. Also, these innovative structures were designed to\\nminimize the tensile stress (maximize the compressive stress) and enable the use of in situ concrete. Our data indicate that compared to our previous study, the\\nmaterial and energy required for construction as well as the construction cost can be reduced by more than 50%. Acceptance criteria and limitations appropriate\\nto the Martian environment, and desirable structural and material behaviors were defined to evaluate whether or not the behavior of a structure under the\\napplied loads and conditions will be acceptable. To detect potential issues for onsite construction and evaluate the geometry of the models, a 1:200 3D model of\\nthe best structural form was printed.\\n\\n### Similar content being viewed by others\\n\\n### [Martian buildings: structural forms using in-place sources](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-25507-5?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access20 December 2022\\n\\n### [Earth analog priors for deployable Mars habitats](https://www.nature.com/articles/s44453-026-00034-z?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access01 June 2026\\n\\n### [Martian differentiation history inferred from copper isotopes](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-64331-z?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access21 October 2025\\n\\n### Subjects\\n- [Astronomy and planetary science](https://www.nature.com/subjects/astronomy-and-planetary-science)\\n- [Engineering](https://www.nature.com/subjects/engineering)\\n- [Civil engineering](https://www.nature.com/subjects/civil-engineering)\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nEarth has experienced five massive extinction events where almost 85% of living species have become extinct in the past 500 million\\nyears[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR1). Some scholars assert that we are now in the midst of the Holocene massive extinction where\\nthe increase in the human population and unchecked devastation of its resources will go beyond Earth’s carrying capacity to rejuvenate itself. Some scientists\\nbelieve that human beings will be forced to seek out energy supplies from other sources such as planets and\\nstars[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#ref-\\nCR2),[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#ref-\\nCR3),[4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR4). A multi-planetary life could be an appropriate answer to such concerns. Additionally, it\\ncould provide economic opportunities such as space tourism, new resources to exploit, exploration of new worlds and help humanity to reach its full\\npotential[5](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR5),[6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR6). Thus, multi-planetary\\nlife seems inevitable and Mars will most likely be our first destination.\\n\\nWith the increase in knowledge and data about Mars and its behavior, studies focusing on the colonization and construction on Mars have accelerated in the\\npresent century[7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR7). The Lunar/Mars Program Office at Johnson Space Center proposed a precast\\nprestressed concrete structure with a diameter of 36 m and height of 22 m (dome-shaped) which tolerated the desired internal pressure for planetary\\nuse[8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR8). NASA studied suitable structural forms for human settlements on Mars for a total mission\\ntime of 919 days (224 days outbound from Earth, 458 days on Mars and 237 days of inbound to Earth)[9](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-\\nCR9). The structure was metal and cylindrical in shape with a diameter of 8 m that could tolerate the internal pressure difference. On such a mission, more than\\n20% of the mission cost would be for the structures/habitats to be imported from Earth.\\n\\nInflatable structures are among the most interesting types of structures proposed for life on Mars. These would be relatively lighter than steel structures and\\neasy to pack and deploy. One of the best examples of inflatable structures is TransHab[10](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR10).\\n\\nThe use of caves and glass domes as human habitats also have been studied. Caves are good for load bearing and could be sealed; however, their availability,\\nsize and conditions on Mars have not been ascertained. They may be a good option for the second stage of\\ncolonization[11](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR11).\"}], \"summaries\": {\"summaries\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d\", \"summary\": \"Focuses on the rationale for lunar in situ construction due to high transport costs and harsh environmental conditions like vacuum pressure differentials, extreme thermal cycles, radiation, and abrasive dust. It outlines the historical shift from heavy Earth-prefabricated modules toward utilizing local regolith, specifically introducing regolith bag structures as a viable approach for sustainable lunar habitat development.\", \"limits\": \"Scope limited to introductory context, environmental challenges, and early conceptual shifts from prefabrication to in situ regolith bag designs; excludes detailed experimental results and structural testing data.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706\", \"summary\": \"Content unavailable due to upstream extraction error.\", \"limits\": \"No substantive content to summarize; restricted to metadata acknowledgment only.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7\", \"summary\": \"Content unavailable due to upstream extraction error.\", \"limits\": \"No substantive content to summarize; restricted to metadata acknowledgment only.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb\", \"summary\": \"Proposes three shape-optimized structural forms (simple, perforated, algorithmic) designed to minimize material, energy, and cost for Martian colonization by leveraging in situ concrete and optimizing geometry to favor compressive over tensile stress. Claims over 50% resource savings compared to prior designs, validated through defined Martian environmental acceptance criteria and a 1:200 scale 3D-printed prototype. Contextualizes these innovations against historical Mars habitat concepts like precast domes, cylindrical habitats, inflatables, and natural caves.\", \"limits\": \"Scope confined to abstract and introduction sections; covers conceptual design rationale, comparative analysis with prior habitat proposals, and initial validation methods, excluding full structural simulations, construction methodologies, and long-term operational data.\"}]}}"}]}
{"type": "model_output", "text": "{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 2\",\n          \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 2\",\n          \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Resolve upstream extraction failures for Martian habitat and robotics sources. Align cited literature with the Mars-focused remaining work, or adjust the direction scope to match the available lunar-centric evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 26\",\n          \"sha256\": \"1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 540\",\n          \"sha256\": \"9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Scale back the immediate trade study scope to a conceptual feasibility review until quantitative data on processing routes, energy/mass budgets, and structural testing are obtained. Supplement with peer-reviewed engineering studies containing empirical trade-off matrices.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 2\",\n          \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 2\",\n          \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Reconstruct the analytical pipeline by retrieving valid text for the Martian habitat and autonomous swarm sources. Bridge the gap between environmental constraints and processing route selection with intermediate technical reports or simulation data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 26\",\n          \"sha256\": \"1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb\",\n          \"locator\": \"line_start: 1, line_end: 540\",\n          \"sha256\": \"9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"recommendation\": \"Generate the required requirements matrix and bounded candidate comparison internally or through targeted literature synthesis before proceeding. The current evidence lacks the structured evaluation criteria and performance benchmarks needed to authorize a baseline architecture.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The direction cannot be admitted in its current form due to critical evidentiary gaps and scope misalignment. Two sources failed to extract, and the remaining materials consist exclusively of introductory contexts and abstract-level conceptual proposals rather than the quantitative processing route data, structural metrics, or trade study matrices mandated by the completion test. Repair requires resolving extraction errors, aligning the lunar/Mars contextual focus, and supplying the detailed engineering analysis and structured evaluation artifacts necessary to execute the trade study.\"\n}", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 4492, "total_tokens": 11521, "completion_tokens": 7029, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 5333, "text_tokens": 7029}}, "model": "qwen3.7-flash"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"direction": {"title": "Regolith Construction Trade Study", "rationale": "Compare processing routes under energy, mass, strength, and maintenance constraints to identify viable pathways for in-situ resource utilization.", "workflow": "engineering_design", "completion_test": "A requirements matrix and bounded candidate comparison that authorizes selection of a baseline architecture for prototyping.", "remaining": "Mars material tests and prototypes to validate structural performance and scalability.", "position": 0}, "sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d", "artifact_id": "artifact:1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427", "sha256": "1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427", "title": "Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction - ScienceDirect", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352710224018138", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 26}, "content": "Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction - ScienceDirect Skip to main contentSkip to article\n## Journal of Building Engineering Date: 15 October 2024 Article: 110245 ## Published by: Elsevier ### Published by Show more Research article Get rights and\ncontent # Conceptual design and experimental investigation of regolith bag structures for lunar in situ construction Charun Bao, Peng Feng, Daobo Zhang, Qinyu\nWang, Shaobo Yang Show more Add to Mendeley More actions ## Highlights ## Keywords Lunar habitat ; In situ construction ; Regolith bag ; Structural design ;\nMultiscale experimental testing - Previous article in this issue - Next article in this issue ## Introduction The aspiration to construct lunar habitats and\nachieve long-term residency has always fueled humanity's drive. As the closest celestial body to Earth and the gateway to deep space exploration for humans, the\nmoon holds significant material, environmental, and positional resources. Establishing permanent lunar habitats is an inevitable choice for lunar scientific\nresearch, resource development, and maintaining space peace and stability. In 2019, the United States introduced the Artemis Program, aiming to establish a\nsustainable presence on the lunar surface within the next decade [1]. In 2021, China and Russia unveiled their plan to construct the International Lunar\nResearch Station (ILRS), focusing on creating scientific research facilities in the lunar polar regions [2,3]. Additionally, Europe, Japan, South Korea, and\nIndia have also announced their intentions for lunar exploration [[4], [5], [6], [7]]. Consequently, the construction of lunar habitats has emerged as a crucial\nengineering requirement and technical challenge in lunar exploration missions. Lunar construction poses formidable challenges, primarily in transporting\nmaterials from the Earth to the moon. This endeavor demands substantial resources, including finances, energy, time, and effort. Other harsh conditions,\ncharacterized by limited energy supplies, the lack of mechanical equipment, and the absence of human assistance, compound the complexity of lunar construction.\nMoreover, the extreme lunar environments introduce unique structural loading considerations. The vacuum environment induces substantial pressure differences in\ninternal and external environments, imposing significant tensile loads on structures. This is a common issue in space structures. Furthermore, lunar temperature\nvariation far exceeds that experienced on the Earth, exposing structures to considerable thermal stresses. In addition, lunar structures must cope with\nheightened radiation levels compared to Earth. Another unique environmental problem is the abrasive and charged characteristics of lunar dust, driven by space\nweathering and solar wind. Furthermore, although rare, micrometeorites and moonquakes introduce potential threats to structures. Micrometeorite impact may cause\nstructural air leaks in a vacuum environment, resulting in catastrophic consequences. In summary, the construction conditions and service environment present\ndistinctive and formidable challenges. Extensive research has been devoted to the construction of inhabited buildings in lunar habitats, as they are regarded as\nthe most crucial and complex construction projects. Over the past six decades, the conceptual scheme for lunar inhabited buildings undergone significant\ndevelopment, primarily shifting from Earth-based prefabrication to in situ construction on the moon. Early construction schemes are generally centered around\nEarth prefabricated structures, such as NASA's LESA integrated module proposed in the 1960s. This module, weighing over 11 tons, was designed to be launched to\nthe moon using the Saturn V Rocket [8]. Similarly, the Soviet Union also proposed a module construction scheme in the Soviet Union Space Projects, planning to\nlaunch nine prefabricated m"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "On the Structural Design and Additive Construction Process of Martian Habitat Units Using In-Situ Resources on Mars", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2226-4310/12/9/761", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7", "artifact_id": "artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6", "title": "Autonomous Robot Swarms for Off-World Construction and Resource Mining", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2514/6.2020-0795", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 2}, "content": "extract_upstream_error\nTarget returned an unsupported response."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb", "artifact_id": "artifact:9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791", "sha256": "9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791", "title": "Sustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete | Scientific Reports", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 540}, "content": "## Sustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete | Scientific Reports\n\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#content)\n\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\nand JavaScript.\n\nSustainable colonization of Mars using shape optimized structures and in situ concrete\n\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9.pdf)\n\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9.pdf)\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe major obstacle to Martian colonization is the mission cost which requires significant reduction. From the structural engineering point of view, importing\nmaterials and structural elements from Earth or massive excavations on the surface of Mars require an enormous amount of energy; thus, inflatable and under-\nsurface structures as the main options for Martian colonization seem unrealistically expensive. Construction of affordable buildings onsite using only in situ\nsources may represent an ideal solution for Martian colonization. On the other hand, solar energy, at the early stage of colonization, would be the only\navailable, practical, and low-cost energy source on Mars. Though, for sustainable and broad colonization, the energy required for construction and the\nconstruction cost should be minimized. Here, we propose three types of simple (relatively optimized), perforated, and algorithmic shape-optimized Martian\nstructures to minimize the material and energy required for construction as well as the construction cost using only in situ resources. These structural forms\ncan be considered remarkable steps towards sustainable structural construction and colonization on Mars. Also, these innovative structures were designed to\nminimize the tensile stress (maximize the compressive stress) and enable the use of in situ concrete. Our data indicate that compared to our previous study, the\nmaterial and energy required for construction as well as the construction cost can be reduced by more than 50%. Acceptance criteria and limitations appropriate\nto the Martian environment, and desirable structural and material behaviors were defined to evaluate whether or not the behavior of a structure under the\napplied loads and conditions will be acceptable. To detect potential issues for onsite construction and evaluate the geometry of the models, a 1:200 3D model of\nthe best structural form was printed.\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\n### [Martian buildings: structural forms using in-place sources](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-25507-5?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access20 December 2022\n\n### [Earth analog priors for deployable Mars habitats](https://www.nature.com/articles/s44453-026-00034-z?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access01 June 2026\n\n### [Martian differentiation history inferred from copper isotopes](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-64331-z?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access21 October 2025\n\n### Subjects\n- [Astronomy and planetary science](https://www.nature.com/subjects/astronomy-and-planetary-science)\n- [Engineering](https://www.nature.com/subjects/engineering)\n- [Civil engineering](https://www.nature.com/subjects/civil-engineering)\n\n## Introduction\n\nEarth has experienced five massive extinction events where almost 85% of living species have become extinct in the past 500 million\nyears[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR1). Some scholars assert that we are now in the midst of the Holocene massive extinction where\nthe increase in the human population and unchecked devastation of its resources will go beyond Earth’s carrying capacity to rejuvenate itself. Some scientists\nbelieve that human beings will be forced to seek out energy supplies from other sources such as planets and\nstars[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#ref-\nCR2),[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=71d0083f-8ad0-4518-8c53-0b031fa959f4#ref-\nCR3),[4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR4). A multi-planetary life could be an appropriate answer to such concerns. Additionally, it\ncould provide economic opportunities such as space tourism, new resources to exploit, exploration of new worlds and help humanity to reach its full\npotential[5](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR5),[6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR6). Thus, multi-planetary\nlife seems inevitable and Mars will most likely be our first destination.\n\nWith the increase in knowledge and data about Mars and its behavior, studies focusing on the colonization and construction on Mars have accelerated in the\npresent century[7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR7). The Lunar/Mars Program Office at Johnson Space Center proposed a precast\nprestressed concrete structure with a diameter of 36 m and height of 22 m (dome-shaped) which tolerated the desired internal pressure for planetary\nuse[8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR8). NASA studied suitable structural forms for human settlements on Mars for a total mission\ntime of 919 days (224 days outbound from Earth, 458 days on Mars and 237 days of inbound to Earth)[9](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-\nCR9). The structure was metal and cylindrical in shape with a diameter of 8 m that could tolerate the internal pressure difference. On such a mission, more than\n20% of the mission cost would be for the structures/habitats to be imported from Earth.\n\nInflatable structures are among the most interesting types of structures proposed for life on Mars. These would be relatively lighter than steel structures and\neasy to pack and deploy. One of the best examples of inflatable structures is TransHab[10](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR10).\n\nThe use of caves and glass domes as human habitats also have been studied. Caves are good for load bearing and could be sealed; however, their availability,\nsize and conditions on Mars have not been ascertained. They may be a good option for the second stage of\ncolonization[11](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-42971-9#ref-CR11)."}], "summaries": {"summaries": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d", "summary": "Focuses on the rationale for lunar in situ construction due to high transport costs and harsh environmental conditions like vacuum pressure differentials, extreme thermal cycles, radiation, and abrasive dust. It outlines the historical shift from heavy Earth-prefabricated modules toward utilizing local regolith, specifically introducing regolith bag structures as a viable approach for sustainable lunar habitat development.", "limits": "Scope limited to introductory context, environmental challenges, and early conceptual shifts from prefabrication to in situ regolith bag designs; excludes detailed experimental results and structural testing data."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706", "summary": "Content unavailable due to upstream extraction error.", "limits": "No substantive content to summarize; restricted to metadata acknowledgment only."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7", "summary": "Content unavailable due to upstream extraction error.", "limits": "No substantive content to summarize; restricted to metadata acknowledgment only."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb", "summary": "Proposes three shape-optimized structural forms (simple, perforated, algorithmic) designed to minimize material, energy, and cost for Martian colonization by leveraging in situ concrete and optimizing geometry to favor compressive over tensile stress. Claims over 50% resource savings compared to prior designs, validated through defined Martian environmental acceptance criteria and a 1:200 scale 3D-printed prototype. Contextualizes these innovations against historical Mars habitat concepts like precast domes, cylindrical habitats, inflatables, and natural caves.", "limits": "Scope confined to abstract and introduction sections; covers conceptual design rationale, comparative analysis with prior habitat proposals, and initial validation methods, excluding full structural simulations, construction methodologies, and long-term operational data."}]}}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 2", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 2", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}], "recommendation": "Resolve upstream extraction failures for Martian habitat and robotics sources. Align cited literature with the Mars-focused remaining work, or adjust the direction scope to match the available lunar-centric evidence."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 26", "sha256": "1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 540", "sha256": "9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791"}], "recommendation": "Scale back the immediate trade study scope to a conceptual feasibility review until quantitative data on processing routes, energy/mass budgets, and structural testing are obtained. Supplement with peer-reviewed engineering studies containing empirical trade-off matrices."}, {"check_id": "ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:e09f695862ba9fb06e65b055442b40b63715f9e879c06afd6d153aedbcd72706", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 2", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:771655a50ab6626e56d9e51549e3c80867e48572d0b162401fb019133ceed7e7", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 2", "sha256": "af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6"}], "recommendation": "Reconstruct the analytical pipeline by retrieving valid text for the Martian habitat and autonomous swarm sources. Bridge the gap between environmental constraints and processing route selection with intermediate technical reports or simulation data."}, {"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:751d1ae51ddf9bbcaf0c668aaa0c42fd9b2f1bbf9bde08856b0a48efb70ff93d", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 26", "sha256": "1535470dc3ef95cec254e95ab1cb75787a5de232db8fe0dae4ec7847f1a4b427"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:97005440cf1168748c14f78f4ec14feb237f07b8382d724a8c063b731e2d34eb", "locator": "line_start: 1, line_end: 540", "sha256": "9f09bfda2b4260f6338143724319d6e30fccd2126065c75c74aa61e819843791"}], "recommendation": "Generate the required requirements matrix and bounded candidate comparison internally or through targeted literature synthesis before proceeding. The current evidence lacks the structured evaluation criteria and performance benchmarks needed to authorize a baseline architecture."}], "summary": "The direction cannot be admitted in its current form due to critical evidentiary gaps and scope misalignment. Two sources failed to extract, and the remaining materials consist exclusively of introductory contexts and abstract-level conceptual proposals rather than the quantitative processing route data, structural metrics, or trade study matrices mandated by the completion test. Repair requires resolving extraction errors, aligning the lunar/Mars contextual focus, and supplying the detailed engineering analysis and structured evaluation artifacts necessary to execute the trade study."}, "tool_receipts": []}}
